if i'm going to make a video explaining  why it is that marvel's on the cusp of  losing spider-man and doctor strange and  a whole host of other characters  to probably never be seen in movie or  comic book form again  i'm going to need a shot of louis 13.  this is going to be an interesting video  okay so first things first we were going  to save this for the room around up and  honestly there's just so much to cover  here and the title is just so damn  clickbait we figured we might as well  make it his own video so what's actually  happening here okay so what's taking  place is that the estates belonging to  various chemical creators stan lee steve  ditko gene cullen among others are  filing lawsuits against marvel to take  back the copyright of the characters  that were created by these various  creators some of which have passed away  now the reason why this matters is  because if that were to happen it could  unfold in two different ways the first  is that they would be able to take back  partial copyright basically meaning they  and disney would share the rights to  these characters and essentially all  future proceeds the second thing that  could happen which is a lot more drastic  is they would get total copyright of  those characters in which case if the  marvel cinematic universe is going to  continue on marvel studios and disney  will actually have to pay them to use  these characters it's a pretty huge  thing now the reason why this is  important and the truth here is a lot of  people are talking about the copyright  act 1976 that's part of it but if we're  really being honest with ourselves this  all goes back to howard the duck so wind  the clock back to 1909 for a brief  second bear with me here back in 1909  with a copyright law as it existed back  then the idea is that if you had person  a making content for person b that  unless there was a clear contract  stating that person a was creating  content for person b as work for hire  then it was assumed that it was work for  hire now there were a few legal  precedents that were created in 1939 and  1965 by virtue of lawsuits but with the  copyright act of 1976  what this did is it basically said that  if person a is creating content for  person b and it's done as work for hire  there has to be a contract specifying  that more so than that if there's not a  contract that specifies that if person b  is spending money to allow person a to  create content for person b then person  b owns the rights to that character  right they're basically it's an expense  and that's the kind of deciding factor  when it comes to this now let's talk  about steve gerber and howard the duck  for a second so steve gerber of course  as you guys know and really just by  context here was a comic book writer for  marvel comics and also for dc the  problem is that he was basically fired  from marvel in the newspaper ships he  was writing at the time because he  couldn't meet deadlines but during his  time writing for marvel he created man  thing and he created howard the duck now  word had reached his ears in 1981 that  marvel wanted to license howard the duck  to create a film which as you guys know  they did and it turned out to be one of  the worst comic book films in the  history of  cinema like in general right but the  important thing here is that steve  gerber filed a lawsuit against marvel  comics to stop them from being able to  license out the film rights of the  character because he argued he was the  sole owner of howard the duck because he  created them now one of the big things  that happened here is yes there were the  clauses of the copyright act of 1976 but  one of the other things that marvel had  done and it was really cadence  industries who owned marvel at the time  is whenever they printed their checks  they had a little legend on the back and  what it said is that the person casting  this check acknowledges they're being  paid as work for hire basically marvel  owns the rights to whatever it is that  they're creating the fact that steve  gerber cashed these checks as far as it  was concerned from a legal precedent is  that steve gerber recognized that by  marvel's own expense he was creating  howard the duck i.e they were paying him  to create a comic book character  therefore marvel owned the rights to  howard the duck he ultimately settled  out of court and he acknowledged this  and that's the precedent that marvel  uses now the idea that's being kicked  around here right now is something  called the marvel method and that's what  marvel is using as this defense because  of the fact that people like stan lee  and steve ditko and gene collin weren't  just sitting in their apartments one day  and said i have an idea for a kid who  basically gets bitten by a radioactive  spider and then i'm going to basically  publish him under marvel comics but i'm  the sole original and only creator of  this that what you had is something  called the marvel method quite literally  you had comic book writers and artists  who would sit in an office called the  bullpen back in the 60s 70s and  partially during the 80s and they would  all basically just kind of spit ball  ideas together but the idea is because  they were working under the umbrella at  marvel in a marvel office and because it  was a collaborative effort among all  these different people all of whom were  performing work for hire by marvel or  for marvel that it was effectively  concepts created and owned by marvel  comics and so following that it was kind  of the standard that they used whenever  they argued those cases and historically  comic book publishers have won there's  never really been an instance where a  comic book publisher has lost the rights  to a particular comic book character  when i say a comic book publisher i mean  like the big two marvel and dc right  there is the legal case of jerry siegel  and joe schuster which was actually them  filing a lawsuit claiming ownership over  superman and it's one of the first  instances when this has actually  happened a kind of a creator filing a  lawsuit claiming copyright over the  character they created now in reality  they sold the rights to superman to dc  and in truth dc came along and basically  paid them a stipend afterward but that  was really more due to social pressure  because people learned that jerry siegel  and joe schuster were basically living  in poverty even though superman was just  like this enormous cash cow and so in  order to basically save face dc ended up  just kind of paying them a stipend for  the rest of their lives until they ended  up passing away but the important thing  here is that in every single instance  where a person or a creator has filed a  lawsuit against a comic book publisher  under the auspices that they own the  character that they basically created  the response of that particular comic  book publisher from the legal side  anyway is no this was work for hire we  paid you to create this character we  paid you to create howard the duck out  of our own pocket or we paid you to  create thor or iron man or black panther  or the mighty avengers or any of these  things regardless the company paid for  that character to be created therefore  under the copyright law of 1976 that  company owns that particular character  now whether or not these guys will  succeed i honestly have no idea only  time will tell but i will bet you this  disney and marvel will not risk losing  this just  multi-billion dollar slush fund right  that's just licensed to print money  they're not going to lose that and  disney's lawyers are nothing if not  litigious and they will comb over every  legal precedent they have to but i am  very curious to see how this ends up  unfolding because if they end up winning  this it'll totally change the landscape  of copyright law not just in relation to  comic books but just in relation to  print media and virtually all forms of  media where a person created something  even though it was done under work for  hire that's now being used by somebody  else even if it does balloon up and  become a multi-billion dollar thing  right but in any event we just wanted to  kind of break this down and explain it  to you guys give you guys a much better  idea of what's going on we'll post  articles and stuff down in the  description so you guys can read it for  yourselves but with that being said guys  thank you all for watching and i will  catch you all later  peace  you

